    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "it's not just grief though. it's like... the math doesn't add up anymore. all the effort, the years, the everything i sacrificed just to end up as a punchline. it feels like there's no logic in trying to \"build something smaller\" when the person who was capable of building anything is gone. why even bother dragging this around if the damage is permanent?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Because \"permanent\" is a word we use when we're too tired to imagine change—not a law of nature. The person who built before is still in there, just buried under the weight of your own judgment. You don't have to rebuild the same monument; you can start with a single brick, even if it's just getting out of bed tomorrow. The damage isn't the end of the story—it's the scar tissue that proves you survived the fire.",
